WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:02.420
We realized kind of intellectually

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:04.020
that to a certain degree

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:04.890
the way we think about this

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.120
planet whether that's water

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.900
on the planet,
life on the planet,

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:09.460
resources on the planet

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:10.880
we tend to think about it

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.520
from the very much
from the context of the surface,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.150
of a 2 dimensional

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:16.420
pale blue dot.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.960
And we realized that if

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.320
we could transform
our way of

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.916
thinking about the planet

00:00:21.916 --> 00:00:22.490
to really

00:00:22.490 --> 00:00:24.240
encompass the challenges

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.580
of three dimensional
science,

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.179
of thinking about what the subsurface

00:00:27.179 --> 00:00:29.800
means in terms of

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.000
exploration for life,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.740
understanding
subsurface life,

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:35.980
understanding water resources,

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.400
understanding the distribution

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.860
of other fluids
and gases

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.320
in the crust

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.720
that this could potentially

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.260
transform how we think

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.820
about the Earth but
most importantly as well

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.310
shed light then on

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:50.590
how we might think about exploring other worlds.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:52.081
One thing that stood out

00:00:52.081 --> 00:00:55.000
is that the surface
of Mars is a hostile place.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.820
It is dry, cold, and it's bad radiation.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.120
So where would you want to be

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:03.550
if you were
a microbe or any form of life?

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.000
You'd want to be underground

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:05.999
is the philosophy.

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:07.520
so that's what led me to

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:08.980
this partnership
with Barb

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:10.950
over the last couple of years because

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.100
here is where the expertise lies

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.140
to really understand

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.319
what a planet like Mars should

00:01:16.319 --> 00:01:17.940
potentially behave like

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.770
and so we're transforming
the knowledge that we're learning from the

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:24.680
Earth and trying to fit that into the what
we understand about Mars.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:31.530
There are not a lot of mechanisms to really
allow that much free-thinking and inter-disciplinarity,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.479
and I think it is that, that CIFAR is going
to be particularly catalytic for us, to be

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:42.490
able to bring together people from extremely
different perspectives and then give them

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:48.030
that intellectual freedom to challenge each
other, challenge themselves to change the thinking.

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:53.859
Having life in this deep, long-term groundwater
on Earth is just opening up an entire field

00:01:53.859 --> 00:01:57.459
of science that we did not know to exist 10
years ago.

00:01:57.459 --> 00:01:58.490
And so what does it mean?

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:04.399
I think we're still trying to map that out,
but in terms of a whole aspect of biology

00:02:04.399 --> 00:02:11.220
that was unknown and how it affects the surface,
how it affects the interior, how biology actually

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:16.489
works within these tight limits is really
the importance of what's going on with the

00:02:16.489 --> 00:02:17.489
life underground.

00:02:17.489 --> 00:02:24.840
[Music]

